B.3 The Tangency Portfolio
Claim. With returns measured in excess of the risk-free rate, the fully invested portfolio of maximum Sharpe ratio is
\[
\boldsymbol{w}_{\text{tan}}
= \frac{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mu}}{B},
\qquad \text{attaining} \qquad
\max_{\boldsymbol{w}} \frac{\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\mu}}
  {\sqrt{\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w}}} = \sqrt{C}.
\]
Proof. The Sharpe ratio \(S(\boldsymbol{w}) = \boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\mu} /
(\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w})^{1/2}\) is homogeneous of degree zero: \(S(c\boldsymbol{w}) = S(\boldsymbol{w})\) for any \(c &gt; 0\). The budget constraint therefore fixes only the scale, not the direction, and we may maximize without it and rescale afterwards. Differentiating,
\[
\frac{\partial S}{\partial \boldsymbol{w}}
= \frac{\boldsymbol{\mu}}{(\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w})^{1/2}}
- \frac{(\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\mu})\,\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w}}
       {(\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w})^{3/2}}
= \boldsymbol{0}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boldsymbol{\mu} = \frac{\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\mu}}
  {\boldsymbol{w}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w}}\,
  \boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{w},
\]
so \(\boldsymbol{w} \propto \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mu}\). Rescaling so that the weights sum to one divides by \(\boldsymbol{\iota}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mu} = B\). Evaluating the ratio at this point gives \(S^2 = (\boldsymbol{\mu}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mu})^2 /
(\boldsymbol{\mu}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mu})
= C\). \(\square\)
Two consequences are worth noting. The solution requires \(B \neq 0\); if \(\boldsymbol{\iota}'\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mu} = 0\) no fully invested tangency portfolio exists, and the ray from the risk-free rate is asymptotic to the frontier rather than touching it. And \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\) enters premultiplied by \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\), which is the algebraic source of the instability documented by Best and Grauer (1991): when \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\) is close to singular, \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1}\) is large, and small changes in estimated means produce large changes in weights.
